# Practical No. 4(B)

## Aim
To implement side effects in a React functional component using the `useEffect` hook.

## Code

### `src/App.js`

```jsx
import React from "react";
import EffectDemo from "./components/EffectDemo";

function App() {
  return <EffectDemo />;
}

export default App;
```

### `src/components/EffectDemo.jsx`

```jsx
import React, { useEffect, useState } from "react";

function EffectDemo() {
  const [name, setName] = useState("");
  const [seconds, setSeconds] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    console.log("Name changed:", name);
  }, [name]);

  useEffect(() => {
    const timer = setInterval(() => {
      setSeconds((value) => value + 1);
    }, 1000);

    return () => clearInterval(timer);
  }, []);

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>useEffect Demo</h2>

      <input
        placeholder="Enter name"
        value={name}
        onChange={(event) => setName(event.target.value)}
      />

      <p>Timer: {seconds} seconds</p>
    </div>
  );
}

export default EffectDemo;
```

## Output

```text
useEffect Demo

[Enter name]

Timer: 0 seconds
```

The timer increases every second, and the entered name is logged in the browser console whenever it changes.

## Result
Successfully implemented side effects using `useEffect` to track name changes and run a timer with proper cleanup.
